      "math_academy_id": "e-13943",
      "problem": "What is the intersection of the intervals shown below, expressed as a number line?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191341/assets/e2da9ff989c1cee3365f88b4ba7bc8dba9ef31f20dc49ae8e4f87a79243473b0.png)",
      "worked_solution": "The intersection of two intervals consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191341/assets/4333901508bff833b854769feabb729b836cdb3aae9827b5170546b2ffbe7d35.png)\n\nTherefore, the number line that shows the common set of values is:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191341/assets/f9d3a9e1da4be61938e41eb5ebe308ed85cd1f60f4fd29f211b2f72071afe159.png)\n\n**Watch out!** The point $-1$ is not included in the intersection since it does not lie in the second interval.",
